     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div>your results are probably ok; if you&#39;ve been looking at the linear.predictor or fitted.values output, they will have been in the order that your index vectors were specified.</div><div>The only issue would be if you also looked at the individual random effect results separately, since they will have been stored in the second type of order (with space index varying fastest, and the time index varying slowly).</div><div><br></div><div>Finn</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Thu, 22 Oct 2020 at 21:46, Insang Song &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">sigma...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hello,</div><div><br></div><div>I am modeling a moderate-sized dataset (3108*14) using a spatiotemporal hierarchical model.</div><div>Until now, I used the temporal index first then spatial index afterwards. After reading other threads in this board, I realized that the index &#39;should&#39; be in spatial-temporal order. For example,</div><div></div><div>My data have had space-time indices as below (in case of spacedim=3, timelength=3)<br></div><div>space  time</div><div>1         1</div><div>1         2</div><div>1         3</div><div>2         1</div><div>2         2</div><div>2         3</div><div>3         1</div><div>3         2</div><div>3         3</div><div><br></div><div>But after referring to the answer for the other thread, the indices should have had:</div><div>space  time</div><div>1         1</div><div>2         1</div><div>3         1</div><div>1         2</div><div>2         2</div><div>3         2</div><div>1         3</div><div>2         3</div><div>3         3</div><div><br></div><div>Thus, my questions would be: 1) I am curious if my modeling was wrong for now. I wonder if the INLA algorithm automatically sort the results by its space-time indices by referring the column names defined in the f() function.</div><div>For your reference, my formula was</div><br><div style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212);background-color:rgb(30,30,30);font-family:Consolas,&quot;Courier New&quot;,monospace;font-weight:normal;font-size:14px;line-height:19px;white-space:pre-wrap\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">form.m42 &lt;- Mortrate </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">~</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\"> </span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">  f(GEOID, model = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(206,145,120)\">&quot;bym&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, graph = graph, constr = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, hyper = hyp.prior) +</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">  f(year1, model = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(206,145,120)\">&#39;ar1&#39;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, constr = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">) +</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">  f(GEOID1, UELAG1, model = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(206,145,120)\">&quot;bym&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, graph = graph, constr = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, hyper = hyp.prior) +</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">  f(year2, UELAG1, model = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(206,145,120)\">&#39;ar1&#39;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, constr = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">) +</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">  f(GEOID2, UELAG1, model = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(206,145,120)\">&quot;bym&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, graph = graph, group = year3, control.group = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">list</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">(model = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(206,145,120)\">&quot;rw1&quot;</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">),</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">    constr = </span><span style=\"color:rgb(86,156,214)\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">, hyper = hyp.prior) + </span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(212,212,212)\">  PELDER + PNONWHITE + MEDINCOME + PUNIVABOVE + PPOVTBELOW + PRURAL + PRENTER + PHSINGLE</span></div><br><br></div><div>In this case, 2) I want to know the INLA model using form.m42 will automatically sort the output by GEOID2 and year3 or others (e.g. GEOID1, year2, etc.).<br></div><div><br></div><div>Thank you.<br></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br clear=\"all\"></blockquote></div><br><div><br></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
